          %h2#idea Idea
          %h3#double_line_notation Double line notation
          %p
            What is called
            %em ’t Hooft double line notation
            (following
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#tHooft74"} ’t Hooft 74
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+representations"} fundamental representations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebras"} semisimple Lie algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+weight+system"} Lie algebra weight
            of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
            with internal/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particle"} virtual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluon"} gluon
            lines is equivalent to one without any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particle"} virtual
            = succeed "-lines," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluon"} gluon
          %p#JacobiIdentity
            1) using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+identity"} Jacobi identity
            to replace all internal gluon vertices by (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+combinations"} linear combinations
            of) quark-gluon vertices
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/QuarkGluonJacobiIdentity.jpg", :width => "580"}/
          %p#MetricContractionOfFundamentalLieAction
            2) replacing any remaining internal gluon line together with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertices"} vertices
            at its ends (equivalently: an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane+3-algebra"} M2-brane 3-algebra
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+combination"} linear combination
            of)
            %em double
            = succeed "-lines," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/MetricActionContraction.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %p#Example
            For example, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{so}(N)
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+Lie+algebra"} special orthogonal Lie algebra
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/tHooftDoubleLineTrivalentVertex.jpg", :width => "640"}/
          %p
            Here we are using the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Penrose+notation"} Penrose notation
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+Lie+representations"} metric Lie representations
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Differential+Cohomotopy+implies+intersecting+brane+observables"} Sati-Schreiber 19c
          %p#Attribution
            %strong Attribution.
            In
            %a{:href => "#tHooft74"} ’t Hooft 74
            this is observed for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+Lie+algebra"} unitary Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{u}(N)
            (see also
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BarNatan95"} Bar-Natan 95 (34)
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ChmutovDuzhinMostovoy11"} Chmutov-Duzhin-Mostovoy 11, p. 177
            %a{:href => "#MetricContractionOfFundamentalLieAction"} above
            appears. The generalization to arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebras"} semisimple Lie algebras
            is observed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Cvitanovic76"} Cvitanović 76, Fig. 14
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+Lie+algebra"} su(N)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+Lie+algebra"} so(N)
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+Lie+algebra"} sp(N)
            in
            %a{:href => "#Cvitanovic08"} Cvitanović 08 (9.57), (10.13) and (12.9)
            and for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+Lie+algebra"} so(N)
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ChmutovDuzhinMostovoy11"} Chmutov-Duzhin-Mostovoy 11, 6.2.6
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{so}(N)
            see also
            %a{:href => "#BarNatan95"} Bar-Natan 95, Section 6.3
            (with an eye towards
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vassiliev+knot+invariants"} Vassiliev knot invariants
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Cicuta82"} Cicuta 82
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ItaNiederOz02"} Ita-Nieder-Oz 02, Figure 3
            %a{:href => "#McGreevySwingle08"} McGreevy-Swingle 08, Figure 10
            (with more discussion of the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+linear+Lie+algebra"} gl(N)
            is considered in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BarNatan96"} Bar-Natan 96, Section 2.2
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ChmutovDuzhinMostovoy11"} Chmutov-Duzhin-Mostovoy 11, 6.2.5
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+linear+Lie+algebra"} sl(N)
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ChmutovDuzhinMostovoy11"} Chmutov-Duzhin-Mostovoy 11, 6.1.8
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#JacksonMoffat19"} Jackson-Moffat 19, Section 14.4
          %h3#surface_notation Surface notation
          %p
            Furthermore, one may regard the resulting double-line diagrams as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+graph"} ribbon graph
            thickening of the original
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surfaces"} surfaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+with+boundary"} with boundary
            and with markings on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
          %p
            In the case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{so}(N)
            this means that a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+particle"} virtual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluon"} gluon
            line is represented by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+linear+combination"} formal linear combination
            of a strip (marked
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disk"} disk
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/tHooftDoubleLineToSurfaces.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %p
            Using this on the reduction of internal gluon vertices by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+identity"} Jacobi identity
            as
            %a{:href => "#JacobiIdentity"} above
            one finds that a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluon"} gluon
            vertex turns into the following linear combination of marked surfaces:
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/tHooftSurfacesForGluonVertex.jpg", :width => "700"}/
          %p#SurfaceExample For example:
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/RisingSuntHooftConstruction.jpg", :width => "620"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Differential+Cohomotopy+implies+intersecting+brane+observables"} Sati-Schreiber 19c
          %p
            This gives
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"} open string
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheets"} worldsheets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            exhibits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory+as+an+open+topological+string+theory"} Chern-Simons theory as an open topological string theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#Witten92"}> Witten 92, see Figures 1 &amp; 2
            ) even for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+N+limit"} small N
          %p
            For
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+duality"} AdS/CFT duality
            relating
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+strings"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            and open to
            %em closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+string+theory"} topological string theory
            (
            %a{:href => "#GopakumarVafa98"}> Gopakumar-Vafa 98
            ) there is an operation of gluing in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/faces"} faces
            to turn these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+strings"} open strings
            into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+string"} closed string
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheets"} worldsheets
            %a{:href => "#GaiottoRastelli03"} Gaiotto-Rastelli 03, Section 1.1
            and see
            %a{:href => "#Marino04"} Marino 04, Section III, p. 14
            for a clear statement).
          %p
            Here
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open%2Fclosed+string+duality"} open/closed string duality
            plays a subtle role in interpreting the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft+double+line+notation"} 't Hooft double line notation
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            alternatively as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"} open string
            or as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+string"} closed string
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheets"} worldsheets
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Gopakumar04"} Gopakumar 04
          %h3#as_a_surfacevalued_weight_system As a surface-valued weight system
          %p
            After averaging over the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n!
            permutations of the ordering of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            external vertices (those on the Wilson line) of a Jacobi diagram, this construction respects the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/STU+relation"} STU relation
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+diagrams"} Jacobi diagrams
            and hence gives a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+system"} weight system
            with values in marked surfaces (
            %a{:href => "#BarNatan95"}> Bar-Natan 95, Theorem 10 with Theorem 8
            ):
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AtHooftConstrictionIII.jpg", :width => "650"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Differential+Cohomotopy+implies+intersecting+brane+observables"} Sati-Schreiber 19c
          %p
            This means that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stringy+weight+systems"} stringy weight systems
            pull back to ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weight+systems"} weight systems
            along this map, corresponding to taking their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} point-particle limit
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stringy+weight+systems+span+classical+Lie+algebra+weight+systems"} stringy weight systems span classical Lie algebra weight systems
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Of course, the ’t Hooft double line construction applies not just to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+diagrams"} Jacobi diagrams
            but also to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sullivan+chord+diagrams"} Sullivan chord diagrams
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/ClosingHorizontalChordsToSullivanChordsIII.jpg", :width => "800"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Differential+Cohomotopy+implies+intersecting+brane+observables"} Sati-Schreiber 19c
          %h2#applications Applications
          %h3#large__limit_and_holography
            Large
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            Limit and holography
          %p
            One upshot of this double-line reformulation is that it makes the dependence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitude"} Feynman amplitude
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            fully explicit, since a double line diagram simply contributes one factor of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            for each closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
            line (this being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            over the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+representation"} fundamental representation
          %p
            In fact, by regarding the resulting double-line diagrams as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+graph"} ribbon graph
            thickenings the original
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+graphs"} ribbon graphs
            in turn as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surfaces"} surfaces
            = succeed "-dependence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus+of+a+surface"} genus
            of these surfaces.
          %p
            One finds this way that in the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit+of+a+sequence"} limit
            where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N \to \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+graphs"} planar graphs
            contribute to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+amplitudes"} Feynman amplitudes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surfaces"} surfaces
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/genus"} genus
            0 (i.e.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tree+level"} tree level
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/planar+limit"} planar limit
            for more on this), while
            = succeed "-corrections" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/N
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
          %p
            Last not least, these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surfaces"} surfaces
            have the interpretation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"} open string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheets"} worldsheets
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            which is “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"}> dual
            ” to the original (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"}> super
            ) in the sense made precise by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+duality"} AdS-CFT duality
          %h3#classification_of_weight_systems Classification of weight systems
          %p
            Later the same double line technique was used (without any reference to the earlier physics articles(?)) in
            %a{:href => "#BarNatan95"} Bar-Natan 95, Section 6
            for discussion of the classification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+weight+systems"} Lie algebra weight systems
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stringy+weight+systems"} stringy weight systems
            with an eye towards discussion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vassiliev+knot+invariants"} Vassiliev knot invariants
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"}> large N limit
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+N+limit"} small N limit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The original article is:
          %ul
            %li#tHooft74
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerard+%E2%80%99t+Hooft"}> Gerard ’t Hooft
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Planar Diagram Theory for Strong Interactions
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/80491"} spire:80491
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(74)90154-0"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(74)90154-0
          %p reviewed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerard+%E2%80%99t+Hooft"}> Gerard ’t Hooft
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Large
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi N
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0204069"} hep-th/0204069
            %li
              %p
                Markus Gross,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Large
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi N
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/GrossLargeN.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Generalization to arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+algebras"} semisimple Lie algebras
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+Lie+groups"}> semisimple Lie groups
            ) is due to:
          %ul
            %li#Cvitanovic76
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Predrag+Cvitanovi%C4%87"}> Predrag Cvitanović
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Group theory for Feynman diagrams in non-Abelian gauge theories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.14.1536"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.14.1536
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/108133"} spire:108133
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/CvitanovicWeights76.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p with a textbook account in
          %ul
            %li#Cvitanovic08
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Predrag+Cvitanovi%C4%87"}> Predrag Cvitanović
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Group Theory: Birdtracks, Lie’s, and Exceptional Groups
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691202983/group-theory"} PUP
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://birdtracks.eu/"} birdtracks.eu
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.birdtracks.eu/version9.0/GroupTheory.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vassiliev+invariants"} Vassiliev invariants
            and the abstract classification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+weight+systems"} Lie algebra weight systems
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stringy+weight+systems"} stringy weight systems
          %ul
            %li#BarNatan95
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dror+Bar-Natan"}> Dror Bar-Natan
                , Section 6 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the Vassiliev knot invariants
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0040-9383(95)93237-2"} doi:10.1016/0040-9383(95)93237-2
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.toronto.edu/drorbn/papers/OnVassiliev/OnVassiliev.pdf"} pdf
            %li#BarNatan96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dror+Bar-Natan"}> Dror Bar-Natan
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Vassiliev and Quantum Invariants of Braids
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9607001"} arxiv:q-alg/9607001
          %p of which textbook accounts are in
          %ul
            %li#ChmutovDuzhinMostovoy11
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergei+Chmutov"}> Sergei Chmutov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sergei+Duzhin"} Sergei Duzhin
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Mostovoy"} Jacob Mostovoy
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to Vassiliev knot invariants
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1103.5628"} arxiv:1103.5628
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139107846"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781139107846
            %li#JacksonMoffat19
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Jackson"}> David Jackson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Iain+Moffat"} Iain Moffat
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Introduction to Quantum and Vassiliev Knot Invariants
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-030-05213-3"} doi:10.1007/978-3-030-05213-3
          %p
            Further discussion of the case of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{so}(N)
            in the context of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
          %ul
            %li#Cicuta82
              %p
                G.M. Cicuta,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Topological Expansion for
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi SO
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(N)
                    and
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Sp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp(2n)
                    Gauge Theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/177713"} spire:177713
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02754653"} doi:10.1007/BF02754653
            %li#ItaNiederOz02
              %p
                Harald Ita, Harald Nieder,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yaron+Oz"} Yaron Oz
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Perturbative Computation of Glueball Superpotentials for
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi SO
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(N)
                    and
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi USp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} USp(N)
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0211261"} arXiv:hep-th/0211261
            %li#McGreevySwingle08
              %p
                McGreevy, Swingle,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    Large
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi N
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                    counting
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/GreevySwingle.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logical+equivalence"} logical equivalence
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/four-colour+theorem"} four-colour theorem
            and a statement about transition from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+N+limit"} small N limit
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra+weight+systems"} Lie algebra weight systems
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+diagrams"} Jacobi diagrams
            via the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft+double+line+construction"} 't Hooft double line construction
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dror+Bar-Natan"}> Dror Bar-Natan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lie Algebras and the Four Color Theorem
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9606016"} arXiv:q-alg/9606016
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01196130"} doi:10.1007/BF01196130
          %h3#for_chernsimons_theory For Chern-Simons theory
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft+double+line+notation"} 't Hooft double line notation
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory+as+topological+string+theory"} Chern-Simons theory as topological string theory
          %ul
            %li#Witten92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Chern-Simons Gauge Theory As A String Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9207094"} arXiv:hep-th/9207094
            %li
              %p
                S. Sinha,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cumrun+Vafa"} Cumrun Vafa
                %em
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi SO
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Sp
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp
                  Chern-Simons at Large
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0012136"}> arXiv:hep-th/0012136
                )
          %h3#openclosed_string_duality Open/closed string duality
          %p
            On the role of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open%2Fclosed+string+duality"} open/closed string duality
            in interpreting the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft+double+line+notation"} 't Hooft double line notation
          %ul
            %li#GopakumarVafa98
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rajesh+Gopakumar"}> Rajesh Gopakumar
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cumrun+Vafa"} Cumrun Vafa
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the Gauge Theory/Geometry Correspondence
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9811131"} arXiv:hep-th/9811131
            %li#GaiottiRastelli05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Davide+Gaiotto"}> Davide Gaiotto
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leonardo+Rastelli"} Leonardo Rastelli
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A paradigm of open/closed duality: Liouville D-branes and the Kontsevich model
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0312196"} hep-th/0312196
          %blockquote
            %p
              Nowadays we interpret
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft+double+line+notation"} 't Hooft double line notation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              quite literally as the perturbative expansion of an open string theory, either because the full open string theory is just equal to the gauge theory (as e.g. for Chern-Simons theory [27]), or because we take an appropriate low-energy limit (as e.g. for N = 4 SYM [31]).
            %p
              The general speculation [1] is that upon summing over the number of holes, (1.1) can be recast as the genus expansion for some closed string theory of coupling
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi s
                      %mo =
                      %msubsup
                        %mi g
                        %mi YM
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_s = g_{YM}^2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N\to \infty
              and tune
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
              to the critical point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_c
              where diagrams with a diverging number of holes dominate.
            %p
              However, in AdS/CFT, or in the Gopakumar-Vafa duality [2],
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
              is a free parameter, corresponding on the closed string theory side to a geometric modulus. The intuition described above clearly goes wrong here.
            %p A much more fitting way in which the open/closed duality may come about in these cases is for each fatgraph of genus g and with h holes to be replaced by a closed Riemann surface of the same genus g and with h punctures: each hole is filled and replaced by a single closed string insertion.
          %ul
            %li#Gopakumar04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rajesh+Gopakumar"}> Rajesh Gopakumar
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Free Field Theory as a String Theory?
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0409233"} hep-th/0409233
            %li#Marino04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcos+Marino"}> Marcos Marino
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Chern-Simons Theory and Topological Strings
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0406005"} arXiv:hep-th/0406005
        .revisedby
